A||N||3||ragilmore||extra pipe||||3||
Z||000000||ragilmore||10-30-2001||04:40 PM||||Iwas looking at the firewall on my 88 formula and I see what seems to be an unused coolant pipe? what gives? thank's Richard ||||reg||3
Z||000001||Scott||10-30-2001||04:52 PM||||It IS an extra pipe, unless your car has a Duke; which uses the pipe to connect heater hoses.  It's used on the 2.8 cars because of the vapor/vacuum line that is attached to the pipe.  I think it's a factory part that was designed for the '84s and never retooled.  But of course, I could be wrong.    <BR>Scott <p>[This message has been edited by Scott (edited 10-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||10-30-2001||05:22 PM||||If it's a steel pipe with one end about mid firewall and the other end running behind the air cleaner, it's left over as part of the recall on the V6 cars. They rerouted the breather tube coming from the valve cover to the hose just ahead of the throttle body. It originally ran to the air cleaner canister. The air cleaner fitting should have a plug on it. I guess they just left the pipe because thet were too cheap to spend a few minutes extra labor on recall work.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||10-30-2001||05:27 PM||||This pipe was disconnected as part of a recall problem. Was replaced by the pipe that goes from your front valve cover to the intake duct, just below/in front of the throttle body. The other end of the (now unused) pipe was connected to the air filter housing. The fitting on the air filter was supposed to be capped off. This was ignored in a lot of cases. <BR>A lot of folks don't realize that the canister of the air filter housing is "filtered" air, and should not be open to the outside world. Make sure you're not sucking in unfiltered air. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Some mornings, it just isn't worth chewing through the restraints.</FONT>||||reg||1
